rticle\n\nDownload PDF\n\nSave article\n\nView saved research\n\nAdvances in Data Analysis and Classification Aims and scope Submit manuscript\n\nThe Frank copula as a latent model for ordinal variables: dependence structure, correlation attenuation, and statistical inference\n\nDownload PDF\n\n  * Alessandro Barbiero  ORCID: orcid.org/0000-0002-5072-5662^{1} \n\n## Abstract\n\nIn this work, we consider the Frank copula, belonging to the Archimedean family and enjoying several interesting properties, and suggest its use as a bivariate and multivariate latent variable for modeling correlated ordinal data, exploring the relationship between its parameter, which models the dependence among the standard uniform components, and the implied (linear) correlation between any pair of ordinal random variables, also as a function of the assigned marginal ordinal distributions. R package version 2.1.0\n\n  * Barbiero A, Hitaj A (2025) Simulating correlated ordinal data via the multivariate Student’s \\(t\\) distribution. In: 6th international conference on data analytics for business and industry (ICDABI2025). pp 322–326\n\n  * Bollen KA, Barb KH (1981) Pearson’s \\(r\\) and coarsely categorized measures. Am Sociol Rev 46(2): 232–239\n\n  * Devroye L, Lugosi G (2001) Combinatorial methods in density estimation. Springer, New York\n\n  * Ferrari PA, Barbiero A (2012) Simulating ordinal data. Multivar Behav Res 47(4):1–24\n\n  * Frank MJ (1978) On the simultaneous associativity of \\(f(x, y)\\) and \\(x+y-f(x, y)\\). Aequ Math 18(1):266–267\n\n  * Gebelein H (1941) Das statistische problem der korrelation als variations-und eigenwertproblem und sein zusammenhang mit der ausgleichsrechnung. ZAMM-J Appl Math Mech/Zeitschrift für Angewandte Mathematik und Mechanik 21(6):364–379\n\n  * Genest C (1987) Frank’s family of bivariate distributions. Biometrika 74(3):549–555\n\n  * Hatzinger R, Maier MJ (2023) prefmod: utilities to fit paired comparison models for preferences. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPackage 'GenOrd' reference manual (https://alessandro-barbiero.r-universe.dev/GenOrd/doc/manual.html)\nciteturn1search10 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Title:  | Simulation of Discrete Random Variables with Marginal Distributions and Correlation Matrix and via a Gaussian or Student's t Copula ... Hitaj (2025) Simulating correlated ordinal data via the multivariate Student's t distribution, 2025 6th International Conference on Data Analytics for Business and Industry (ICDABI)\nThe procedure can handle both Pearson and Spearman correlations, and any finite support for the discrete variables. The intermediate function `contord` computes the correlations of the multivariate discrete variable derived from discretization of a multivariate normal or Student's t variable. Function `corrcheck` returns the lower and upper bounds of the correlation coefficient of each pair of discrete variables given their marginal distributions, i.e., returns the range of feasible bivariate correlations. Function `estcontord`, to be used with bivariate samples only, estimates the parameters of the Gaussian/t copula and possibly of the margins.\n\nCompared to version 2.0.0, this version has refined the optimization routine and t probability calculation involved in the estimation process.\n\n### Details\n\nPackage:  | GenOrd\nType:  | Package\nVersion:  | 2.1.0\nDate:  | 2026-06-30\nLicense:  | GPL\nLazyLoad:  | yes\n\n### Author(s)\n\nAlessandro Barbiero, Pier Alda Ferrari\n\nMaintainer: Alessandro Barbiero <[email protected]>\n\n### References\n\nP.A. Ferrari, A. Barbiero (2012) Simulating ordinal data, Multivariate Behavioral Research, 47(4), 566-589\n\nA. Barbiero, P.A. Ferrari (2015) Simulation of correlated Poisson variables. Applied Stochastic Models in Business and Industry, 31(5), 669-680.\n\nA. Barbiero, P.A. Ferrari (2017) An R package for the simulation of correlated discrete variables. Communications in Statistics-Simulation and Computation, 46(7), 5123-5140.\n\nA. Barbiero, A. Hitaj (2025) Simulating correlated ordinal data via the multivariate Student's t distribution, 2025 6th International Conference on Data Analytics for Business and Industry (ICDABI)\n\n### See Also\n\n`contord`, `ordcont`, `corrcheck`, `ordsample`, `estcontord`\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNaviga IRIS (https://air.unimi.it/browse?authority=rp17531&etal=-1&offset=100&order=ASC&rpp=20&sort_by=2&starts_with=&type=author)\nciteturn1search11 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; Barbiero Maximal point‐polyserial correlation for non‐normal random distributions 2025 A. ... Hitaj Simulating correlated ordinal data via the multivariate Student’s t distribution 2026 A.\nBarbiero, A. Hitaj】 A discrete Kumaraswamy distribution for modeling rating and ranking data 2026 A. Barbiero, A. Hitaj A collective risk model with shared latent variable inducing dependence among claim frequency and sizes 2026 A. Barbiero, A. Hitaj Simulating correlated ordinal data via the multivariate Student’s t distribution 2026 A. Barbiero, A. Hitaj\n\nTitolo  | Data di pubblicazione  | Autori  | Tipo  | File  | Abstract\n--- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nMinimizing distance between distribution functions: discrete counterparts to continuous random variables with applications in non-life insurance and stochastic reliability  | 2024  | Barbiero, AlessandroHitaj, Asmerilda + -  | Article (author)  |  | -\nChoosing among different discrete approximations of a continuous random variable  | 2024  | A. BarbieroAsmerilda Hitaj + -  | Book Part (author)  |  | -\nYet another approximation for the total claims amount using the Weibull distribution  | 2024  | Alessandro Barbiero  | Conference Object  |  | -\nModeling correlated ordinal data through Student's t copula  | 2025  | A. Barbiero  | Book Part (author)  |  | -\nOn the Ratio Between Point-Polyserial a